<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John III, Duke of Cleves</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1490">1490</a>–<a href="/years/1539">1539</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as John the Peaceful · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-la-marck">House of La Marck</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John III, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kleve">Cleves</a>, called the Peaceful, married Maria, heiress of Jülich and Berg. On inheriting from his father and father-in-law by 1521 he united Cleves, Mark, Jülich, Berg and Ravensberg into the United Duchies, a major power of the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>. He followed a humanist middle way in religion, and his daughter Anne married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII of England</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-iii-duke-of-cleves.jpg" alt="John III, Duke of Cleves" width="320" height="382" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Johann_III_von_Kleve-J%C3%BClich-Berg.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>John III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kleve">Cleves</a>, known as John the Peaceful, was born on 10 November 1490, the son of John II, Duke of Cleves, and Mathilde of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>. In 1510, at the age of 19, he married Duchess Maria of Jülich-Berg, daughter of Duke William IV of Jülich-Berg and Sibylle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>, who became heiress to her father's estates of Jülich, Berg and Ravensberg. He was also Count of Mark. In 1521 he became ruler of the United Duchies of Jülich-Cleves-Berg, which he is regarded as having founded, and in 1528 Lord of Ravensberg.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>During the Protestant Reformation John took a compensatory attitude and sought a middle way between the two confessions. The real influence at his court was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erasmus">Erasmus</a>, and many of his men were friends and followers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> scholar and theologian. In 1532 John drew up a list of church regulations, a Kirchenordnung, that expressed many of Erasmus's ideas. He had an instinct for balance, shown when he married his eldest daughter Sibylle to John Frederick, elector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, who later headed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schmalkaldic-league">Schmalkaldic League</a>. His court was fundamentally liberal, serious-minded, theologically inclined and strongly Erasmian, and it was there that his daughter Anne was raised.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>John and Maria had four children: Sibylle (1512–1554), Anne (1515–1557), William (1516–1592) and Amalia (1517–1586). Anne became the fourth wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII of England</a>; the marriage was annulled on 12 July 1540, after John's death, on the grounds of non-consummation and a pre-contract to Francis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>. William married Archduchess Maria, daughter of Emperor Ferdinand I, and Amalia became a princess of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-la-marck">House of La Marck</a>. John also had a daughter, Elisabeth, with an unknown woman, and another daughter who became a nun. He died on 6 February 1539.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “John III of Cleves” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1512, during the Italian Wars, Ferdinand the Catholic accused the Albret monarchs, <b>John III</b> and Catherine of Foix, of siding with France and sent the Duke of Alba to seize Pamplona.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-navarre">Kingdom of Navarre · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1521 Duke <b>John III</b> united Cleves with Jülich and Berg, forming the United Duchies.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-cleves">Duchy of Cleves</a></li><li><blockquote><b>John III</b> of Cleves-Mark married Maria, heiress of Jülich, Berg and Ravensberg.</blockquote><a href="/state/united-duchies-of-julich-cleves-berg">United Duchies of Jülich-Cleves-Berg</a></li></ul></section>
